<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Caxton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1422–1491</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Caxton was an English merchant, translator and printer who introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. After learning the trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, where he printed the first book in English, he set up a press at Westminster in 1476. He printed around a hundred books, among them Chaucer's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> Tales and Malory's Le Morte d'Arthur, helping to standardise written English.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-caxton.jpg" alt="William Caxton" width="320" height="484" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raoul Lefèvre / William Caxton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caxton-Margaret-of-York-Histoires_de_Troie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
